<p><strong>A business keeps consent organised when each client's form is collected before the appointment, stored against their booking and easy to find later, instead of lying in a drawer.</strong></p>
@NOTE@@
<h2>The form nobody can find</h2>
<p>A client returns for a touch-up and the studio cannot find the form she signed last year. Another fills in an intake sheet at the counter with a pen that does not work, while the artist waits. The paper copy has coffee on it.</p>
<h2>Why paper consent causes problems</h2>
<ul><li>Forms get lost, damaged or filled in hurriedly.</li><li>Nothing links the form to the booking.</li><li>Finding one later means searching a box.</li></ul>
<h2>How to manage consent better</h2>
<ol><li><strong>Send the form before the visit,</strong> so it is read calmly.</li><li><strong>Store it against the client and booking,</strong> not in a separate pile.</li><li><strong>Keep the version that was signed</strong> and the date.</li><li><strong>Check your local rules</strong> for what to collect and keep. This is a general guide, not legal advice.</li></ol>
